<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conservatism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1790 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conservatism is a philosophy and ideology that seeks to preserve traditional institutions, customs and values, founded as a modern tradition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a> in his opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Anglo-Irish statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>, who opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, is credited as one of the forefathers of Western conservative thought in the 1790s, along with the Savoyard statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-de-maistre">Joseph de Maistre</a>. Burke served as private secretary to the Marquess of Rockingham and as official pamphleteer to the Rockingham branch of the Whig party, which together with the Tories made up the conservatives of the late 18th century United Kingdom. The first established use of the term in a political context came in 1818 from François-René de Chateaubriand, during the Bourbon Restoration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Conservatism seeks to promote and preserve traditional institutions, customs and values, and its central tenets vary with the culture in which it appears. In Western countries conservatives have sought to preserve institutions such as the family, organised religion, the nation-state, property rights, aristocracy and monarchy. Political scientist Andrew Heywood names five central beliefs: tradition, human imperfection, organic society, authority and hierarchy, and property. The philosopher Michael Oakeshott wrote that to be conservative is to prefer the familiar to the unknown and the tried to the untried.</p><p>The ideology has many variants. Paternalistic conservatism first arose in the United Kingdom under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a>'s One Nation Toryism and stresses the obligation of the privileged towards the poor. Liberal conservatism joins a minimal role for the state in the economy to a strong state for law and order. Reactionary conservatism seeks to return to an earlier state of affairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early European forms of conservatism were often reactionary and romantic, idealising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> with their feudal order, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristocracy">aristocratic rule</a> and established church. In the late 19th century conservatism became more progressive, adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and nationalism, which until then had been anti-traditionalist forces. In the first half of the 20th century, as socialist movements grew stronger and the Tsarist regime fell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>, conservatism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> moved to the far right and became more authoritarian. After the war it took a more moderate form, with Christian-democratic parties dominating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> conservative elites held power through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and the military.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conservatism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He was a man of deep personal <b>conservatism</b> and tenacity, and he held together a construction that tended to pull apart through longevity and bureaucratic discipline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>But unrest at home and abroad, which the tsar blamed on political liberalisation, led to a series of repressions and a return to <b>conservatism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…as an extreme ethnic nationalism differing from fascism in not fusing the movement with its leader and in placing society above the state and race, Chetan Bhatt and Parita Mukta call it “revolutionary <b>conservatism</b>” or “ethnic absolutism”, and Achin Vanaik denies it belongs to the genus of fascism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>In Europe and Latin America it covers both the conservative liberalism of the centre-right and the social liberalism of the centre-left; in North America it almost always means social liberalism, and in the United States the ideas of classical liberalism passed into modern <b>conservatism</b> and libertarianism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1975 Ronald Reagan, then governor of California, told Reason he believed “the very heart and soul of <b>conservatism</b> is libertarianism”, and Ron Paul supported his 1980 campaign.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/libertarianism">Libertarianism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>There are various definitions of a nation, so there are different types of nationalism, and it can be combined with <b>conservatism</b> or with socialism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
